  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Server Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the server and API logic, specifically focusing on LokalSwap\'s PRD. The initial focus is the file upload and extraction endpoint (`POST /api/extract`). I\'m looking at how it handles `.pdf`, `.docx`, and `.txt` files through multipart uploads or JSON base64.\n\n\n**Refining Localization Process**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `POST /api/localize` endpoint. The first phase, cultural context swapping, is underway, and I\'m leveraging `@google/genai` with `gemini-3.6-flash`. I am focusing on replacing generic/foreign elements with region-specific equivalents, while ensuring pedagogical structure and equations remain untouched, adjusting for currency/contextual items. I\'m focusing on the input/output format.\n\n\n**Integrating Translation Step**\n\nI\'ve integrated the dialect translation step into the workflow. The system prompt handles the translation of culturally localized text into the specified Philippine regional language. The output is now in JSON format: `{ translated_text, notes }`. I am thinking about how this can be combined into a single, clean API response that is easy to manage, with the following format: `{ original, localized, changes, translation: { text, language, notes } }`.\n\n\n**Handling API Failures**\n\nI\'m now implementing robust error handling for the API calls to ensure graceful fallbacks when the `GEMINI_API_KEY` is missing or the AI call fails. I am designing the structured output to maintain application functionality, even with AI interruptions.\n\n**Defining Region Data**\n\nNext, I\'ve started defining the data structures for regions and language options, aiming for rich, contextual presets. I am using a detailed list of regional options, including local landmarks, common transport, regional delicacies, and relevant currency. I\'m focusing on providing context-specific details.\n\n\n**Mapping Region Data**\n\nI\'m now mapping out region-specific data, focusing on rich contextual presets. This includes landmarks, transport, local cuisine, and currency like *\342\202\261 pesos*. I\'ve defined presets such as Leyte-Tacloban, with Tacloban Supermarket and the San Juanico Bridge. I\'m also cataloging target dialects/mother tongue languages such as Waray-Waray and Hiligaynon.\n\nI\'m now reviewing how the front-end will display and handle the Diff Viewer. I\'m thinking through side-by-side views: original vs. localized, with green highlights for changes that open inline editors on click. I\'m focusing on features such as clean typography, line counts, and clear headers.\n\n\n**Organizing the UI**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the Diff Viewer and its integration. I\'ve fleshed out the idea of clickable spans that transform into inline input fields for customizing replacements. There will be an immediate update to the right pane text, along with an instant badge update. Also, I\'ve designed the Dialect Translation Card with a target language badge, editability, and copy features below the Diff Viewer. Finally, I\'ve designed the top bar with branding and quick actions. The export options, including copying localized text, translations, and download choices are being outlined.\n\n\n**Structuring the Front-End**\n\nI\'m now implementing the UI components and data structures. I have the types defined in `/src/types.ts`. I have pre-configured regions and languages in `/src/data/regionsAndLanguages.ts`. I\'m using `/src/utils/diffUtils.ts` to parse and render highlighted spans, which is critical. I\'ve designed header, upload section, diff viewer, translation card, and export options, using dedicated components in the `src/components` folder. I\'m making progress on the Express server (`server.ts`).\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 480940439
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
