  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Clarifying Contextualization Flow**\n\nI\'m focusing on the technical walkthrough for the contextualization flow in LokalSwap, providing clear and concise answers. I will structure the explanation using bullet points to delineate the two-step AI process.\n\n\n**Analyzing Technical Flow**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the technical flow, concentrating on the pipeline, specifically the document extraction layer and diff rendering engine, while avoiding external tool calls or file modifications. I\'ve re-examined the `server.ts` code and frontend components to verify document ingestion, which supports PDF, DOCX, and TXT files, and also accepts pasted text. The code employs `multer` for buffering, `pdf-parse` for PDFs, and `mammoth` for DOCX files.\n\n\n**Refining Localization Process**\n\nI\'m now refining the localization process. Step one focuses on cultural swapping: adapting context like location (`Naga People\'s Mall`), transit (`subway` \342\206\222 `tricycle`), and currency (`$2` \342\206\222 `\342\202\26120`), and names (`Mrs. Johnson` \342\206\222 `Aling Maria`). I am preserving math equations and learning objectives. Step two is translation to the regional dialect, like Central Bikol, done with Gemini 2.0 Flash, targeting conversational phrasing for MTB-MLE classrooms. A fallback engine for offline functionality has been included.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "Here is a technical walkthrough of how the contextualization pipeline operates in **LokalSwap**, from file upload to the visual green diff:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Document Extraction Layer (`POST /api/extract`)\n\n1. **Upload Handling**:\n   - The React frontend receives `.pdf`, `.docx`, or `.txt` files via drag-and-drop or file picker.\n   - Files are sent to `/api/extract` as `multipart/form-data` and parsed directly in Node.js memory via `multer`.\n2. **Format Parsing**:\n   - **PDFs**: Parsed using `pdf-parse` to extract plain text layout streams.\n   - **Word Docs**: Parsed using `mammoth` to extract clean document text without binary XML bloat.\n   - **Text Files**: Streamed directly into UTF-8 strings.\n3. The extracted text is returned and previewed in the frontend textarea prior to processing.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Two-Step AI Pipeline (`POST /api/localize`)\n\nTo ensure high reliability, the contextualization process is split into **two distinct LLM passes** powered by **Gemini 2.0 Flash** (`gemini-3.6-flash`) using strict JSON schema enforcement (`responseMimeType: \"application/json\"`):\n\n```\nUploaded Lesson Plan\n        \342\224\202\n        \342\226\274\n[ Step 1: Cultural Entity Swap ]  \342\224\200\342\224\200>  Outputs: Localized Text + Substitution Array\n        \342\224\202\n        \342\226\274\n[ Step 2: Mother Tongue Pass ]    \342\224\200\342\224\200>  Outputs: Regional Dialect Translation\n```\n\n#### Step 1: Cultural Entity Swap Pass\n* **Goal**: Replace culturally foreign entities (places, transport, currency, food, names) with realistic equivalents tailored to the target Philippine region (e.g., Bicol / Naga City, Cebu, Davao, Ilocos).\n* **Guarantees**:\n  - The model **only** replaces entities (`subway` \342\206\222 `tricycle`, `Walmart` \342\206\222 `Naga People\'s Mall`, `$2` \342\206\222 `\342\202\26120`, `apples` \342\206\222 `saging`).\n  - Mathematical values, equations, learning objectives, and pedagogical logic remain **100% untouched**.\n  - Returns a structured JSON payload containing `localized_text` and a `changes` array listing every `{ original, replacement, category }`.\n\n#### Step 2: Dialect Translation Pass\n* **Goal**: Translate the culturally swapped scenario into the selected mother tongue (e.g., Central Bikol, Cebuano, Ilocano, Hiligaynon, Waray) for DepEd MTB-MLE classroom instruction.\n* **Separation Rationale**: Keeping translation as a secondary pass ensures the English/Filipino green diff is always mathematically and contextually accurate \342\200\224 even if a teacher chooses to customize the dialect translation manually.\n\n> **Offline / Keyless Fallback Engine**: If no Gemini API key is detected or a network error occurs, a local deterministic regex engine executes regional substitutions instantly so the app remains demo-ready.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Visual Diff & Interactive Review Engine (`DiffViewer.tsx` & `diffUtils.ts`)\n\n1. **Segment Indexing**:\n   - `buildDiffSegments()` parses the localized text against the `changes` ledger to create position-aware text segments.\n2. **Categorized Highlighting**:\n   - Unchanged text renders as standard typography.\n   - Substituted entities render as interactive green badges (`bg-emerald-100`, `border-emerald-500`) tagged by category (*Transport*, *Store*, *Currency*, *Food*, *Name*).\n3. **Teacher-in-the-Loop Override**:\n   - Clicking any green badge opens an edit popover where teachers can customize the replacement or revert it to the original term. Updating a substitution immediately re-indexes the diff and translation card in real time.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Export & Package Layer (`ExportSection.tsx`)\n\nTeachers can copy either the localized text or dialect pass with a single click, or download a consolidated `.txt` package containing:\n1. Contextualized Regional Lesson Plan\n2. Mother Tongue Dialect Pass\n3. Substitution Ledger\n4. Original Source Text"
